<!--
  Upstream's clickhouse/low-resources.xml, sized down further. Upstream targets
  a machine with a few gigabytes to spare and caps the mark cache at 500 MB;
  this ClickHouse has a 768 MB mem_limit inside a 2.5 GB account shared with
  Plausible and PostgreSQL.

  max_server_memory_usage is stated rather than left to the default ratio of
  the *host's* RAM: ClickHouse does read the cgroup limit, but saying the
  number leaves nothing to a kernel interface that has changed shape between
  cgroup v1 and v2.

  The schedule pools are sized down because they are allocated eagerly and the
  account's containers have a pids limit. BackgroundSchedulePool's constructor
  starts all of its threads at once, and the default is 512 of them — more than
  the 256 pids ServiceHardener gives a service, so ClickHouse aborted at
  startup with "Couldn't get 512 threads from global thread pool". The compose
  file raises this container's pids_limit as well; both are needed, because a
  container that may create a thousand threads still should not.

  background_pool_size is deliberately left at its default. Cutting it to 4 is
  the obvious next saving and it refuses to start the other way:
  `number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_mutation` defaults to 20 and is
  validated against background_pool_size *
  background_merges_mutations_concurrency_ratio, so a pool of 4 fails with
  BAD_ARGUMENTS before the server ever listens.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <max_server_memory_usage>536870912</max_server_memory_usage>
    <mark_cache_size>134217728</mark_cache_size>
    <max_concurrent_queries>16</max_concurrent_queries>
    <background_schedule_pool_size>32</background_schedule_pool_size>
    <background_distributed_schedule_pool_size>8</background_distributed_schedule_pool_size>
    <background_message_broker_schedule_pool_size>8</background_message_broker_schedule_pool_size>
    <mlock_executable>false</mlock_executable>
</clickhouse>
